To fully understand
 my solution to the challenge,

00:00:49.541 --> 00:00:53.833
you should first watch a video on
 LifeOverflow's channel where he explains

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.666
how the challenge is built
 and what are the components.

00:00:56.833 --> 00:00:59.208
I will mainly focus on hacking it.

00:00:59.541 --> 00:01:03.833
Right now click the card or the link
 in the description to watch that video.

00:01:04.916 --> 00:01:06.500
I think he's a guy that 

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:10.916
doesn't need introduction,
 but in case you missed it, he's a YouTuber

00:01:10.958 --> 00:01:13.916
with over 600,000 subscribers

00:01:13.916 --> 00:01:17.583
and over 300 public IT security videos.

00:01:17.875 --> 00:01:21.791
There's a whole range of topics
 covered from web security,

00:01:21.791 --> 00:01:26.083
through CTFs to low-level
 and even hardware exploitation.

00:01:26.375 --> 00:01:30.708
If you are not subscribed or YouTube
 does no longer recommend you his videos,

00:01:30.916 --> 00:01:34.083
check out tons of free,
 high-quality content.

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:37.125
Did you watch it already? 

00:01:37.125 --> 00:01:40.166
I hope, yes, so we can go ahead
 with the walkthrough.

00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:43.166
Let's quickly see
 what the application does.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:45.583
There are two separate directories.

00:01:45.708 --> 00:01:48.541
First, I took a look at the app itself.

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:50.875
When seeing the source code, 

00:01:50.875 --> 00:01:54.541
I did not spend too much time
 on those helper functions.

00:01:54.833 --> 00:01:58.083
Instead, I went straight
 down to controllers.

00:01:59.208 --> 00:02:00.416
In most of them, 

00:02:00.416 --> 00:02:03.791
queries are executed
 using prepared statements.

00:02:04.333 --> 00:02:09.958
It means that the application knows those
 parameters are not trusted and SQL

00:02:09.958 --> 00:02:14.791
injection won't happen in them unless,
 of course, there's a bug in the library.

00:02:15.958 --> 00:02:20.958
However, in one function
 there is a query created the unsafe way.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:23.666
The prepared statement is used

00:02:23.666 --> 00:02:27.375
but the order argument
 is not a statement's parameter.

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:32.291
Python creates the string before
 even calling query_db.

00:02:32.916 --> 00:02:36.041
The function considers
 this string as trusted,

00:02:36.041 --> 00:02:39.875
including the order parameter
 which is controlled by the user.

00:02:40.791 --> 00:02:43.666
Every time there is just one place

00:02:43.666 --> 00:02:46.875
where something is done
 differently, is a good lead.

00:02:47.458 --> 00:02:52.750
Of course, there is a chance that cheeky LifeOverflow planted this as a red herring,

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:55.541
but one way or another,
 I had to check this.

00:02:56.291 --> 00:02:58.458
I launched the application locally.

00:02:59.333 --> 00:03:02.000
I quickly confirmed SQL injection

00:03:02.083 --> 00:03:05.958
by using asc limit 0 and I saw no notes.

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:10.208
It meant that my limit clause
 was present in the SQL

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:12.583
query and it returned zero rows.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:15.666
Then I tried with the union.

00:03:16.416 --> 00:03:19.708
However, I got blocked by web
 application firewall.

00:03:20.208 --> 00:03:25.083
At this point, I had to understand the
 relationship between the two applications.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.500
If you did watch
 LifeOverflow's video you know it.

00:03:29.666 --> 00:03:34.708
The crypter works like a reverse proxy,
 and the app itself is not exposed.

00:03:34.958 --> 00:03:39.000
I found that out
 by examining contents of Dockerfiles.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:42.833
Inside the source code,
 there is only one controller

00:03:42.833 --> 00:03:47.833
which forwards the requests to the app,
 but before doing that,

00:03:47.958 --> 00:03:51.583
the was_param function was called
 on all the parameters.

00:03:52.458 --> 00:03:54.625
Its logic was very simple. 

00:03:54.625 --> 00:03:57.458
It filtered only
 a few words or characters.

00:03:57.791 --> 00:04:00.875
Those included select, union and quotes.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.416
It's a short list,
 but it seemed quite convenient to me.

00:04:05.833 --> 00:04:10.875
There was also no unusual things that
 could open a way to more advanced attacks.

00:04:11.416 --> 00:04:16.791
So I wanted to find a way to smuggle my
 parameters without entering this function.

00:04:17.583 --> 00:04:20.791
I spent some time here
 by trying to find the bypasses

00:04:20.791 --> 00:04:24.958
using those unfiltered values
 like content-type header or cookie header,

00:04:25.208 --> 00:04:28.208
but I didn't come up with anything smart.

00:04:29.125 --> 00:04:31.916
Then I thought about
 spending a bit more time

00:04:32.125 --> 00:04:34.625
on the way those URLs are created.

00:04:35.083 --> 00:04:38.500
Every time I see an address
 just concatenated like this,

00:04:38.708 --> 00:04:40.958
I feel like something might be off.

00:04:42.125 --> 00:04:44.208
And I was right this time. 

00:04:44.208 --> 00:04:47.041
The path variable is a URL parameter.

00:04:47.625 --> 00:04:52.958
There are no explicit transformations
 here, but Flask automatically decodes it.

00:04:53.625 --> 00:04:57.250
So if I used a path
 with URL-encoded question mark,

00:04:57.458 --> 00:05:01.000
then the request was sent
 with the question mark decoded.

00:05:01.541 --> 00:05:04.458
The reverse proxy didn't consider those

00:05:04.458 --> 00:05:07.541
to be parameters,
 but rather a part of the path.

00:05:07.833 --> 00:05:12.916
So this way I was able to smuggle my
 payloads, bypassing the WAF.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.250
Note the ampersand at the end.

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:21.083
It's important because if it wasn't there,
 the question mark would be treated

00:05:21.083 --> 00:05:24.833
as the part of the parameter,
 breaking my SQL query.

00:05:25.875 --> 00:05:30.708
I confirmed that this bypass works
 and I was ready to create the exploit.

00:05:31.208 --> 00:05:33.458
I took a look into where the flag is.

00:05:33.583 --> 00:05:35.666
It was added as the first note.

00:05:36.583 --> 00:05:38.666
I wanted to use sqlmap for it

00:05:38.666 --> 00:05:42.125
because it's much faster
 when it comes to exfiltrating data,

00:05:42.666 --> 00:05:46.458
but I couldn't configure it
 to work with my specific case

00:05:46.458 --> 00:05:48.916
where the payload was
 the part of the path.

00:05:49.916 --> 00:05:53.416
I didn't think creating tamper script
 would help either.

00:05:53.958 --> 00:05:57.125
Modifying the source code was an option
 that I considered,

00:05:57.375 --> 00:06:00.333
but they opted to write
 my own exploit instead.

00:06:01.708 --> 00:06:06.833
The first step is determining
 what query to use to extract the flag.

00:06:07.791 --> 00:06:09.875
Testing this on a running web app 

00:06:09.875 --> 00:06:13.916
would be very inefficient way,
 even if it was running locally.

00:06:14.208 --> 00:06:18.500
I would have to make additional effort
 in order not to break encoding.

00:06:19.833 --> 00:06:24.666
Instead, I took an advantage of the fact
 that this is an open source task.

00:06:25.166 --> 00:06:28.666
I just copied the database file
 from the Docker container

00:06:28.666 --> 00:06:30.791
and started testing different queries.

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:36.416
At this point, I noticed that there is
 no plaintext flag in the database.

00:06:36.666 --> 00:06:39.083
Instead, it's encrypted. 

00:06:39.083 --> 00:06:43.208
Considering the name of the challenge,
 I could have expected this.

00:06:44.083 --> 00:06:47.041
It was time to understand
 the logic of this WAF.

00:06:47.708 --> 00:06:50.125
Again, I was just briefly mention it in

00:06:50.125 --> 00:06:53.125
the full explanation
 is on LifeOverflow's channel.

00:06:53.875 --> 00:06:56.166
I didn't find anything out of place

00:06:56.166 --> 00:06:59.833
in generating a secret
 or encryption/decryption functions.

00:07:00.875 --> 00:07:04.291
What was interesting,
 however, was that when ciphering,

00:07:04.291 --> 00:07:07.916
the string ENCRYPTED:
 was prepended to the content.

00:07:07.916 --> 00:07:11.208
Most POST and GET params were ciphered.

00:07:12.166 --> 00:07:16.083
Then, the decrypt
 function took the whole response body

00:07:16.083 --> 00:07:20.625
and deciphered everything
 that had the string ENCRYPTED: prepended.

00:07:21.416 --> 00:07:26.541
So my idea was to get the encrypted
 input anywhere in the response.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:29.375
Of course, it couldn't be in the note

00:07:29.666 --> 00:07:32.750
because then it would
 simply be encrypted twice.

00:07:34.125 --> 00:07:37.958
However, there were some inputs
 that weren't encrypted,

00:07:37.958 --> 00:07:41.666
and those were very good candidates
 for this purpose.

00:07:42.916 --> 00:07:46.333
I quickly scanned the controller
 for the presence of those

00:07:46.333 --> 00:07:51.458
first three parameters,
 but only so uuid used and in the redirect.

00:07:51.791 --> 00:07:53.833
So I quickly dismissed the idea.

00:07:54.625 --> 00:07:59.291
I thought, why not create an account
 with the encrypted flag as the username?

00:07:59.833 --> 00:08:01.500
And it did work. 

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:05.541
I created a user
 using the extracted, encrypted flag.

00:08:05.833 --> 00:08:10.291
And what I saw after the login
 was the flag from my local environment.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:14.083
So I had the encryption
 aspect taken care of.

00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:19.541
Now, how to get this
 encrypted flag out of the database?

00:08:20.625 --> 00:08:24.583
At first I tried the simple union,
 but it didn't work.

00:08:25.083 --> 00:08:30.375
It was because Union mustn't be after
 the order clause in SQL query.

00:08:31.333 --> 00:08:33.958
So this is a blind SQL injection.

00:08:34.583 --> 00:08:38.583
I couldn't use union,
 but what I could do is a subquery.

00:08:39.666 --> 00:08:43.833
That means that you can do
 arbitrary queries to the database,

00:08:43.833 --> 00:08:46.833
but you just can't
 return them as the result.

00:08:47.416 --> 00:08:52.250
There are many methods which you can use
 to exfiltrate one bit at a time.

00:08:52.708 --> 00:08:56.291
And if you can exfiltrate one bit,
 you can exfiltrate

00:08:56.291 --> 00:08:58.583
the whole flag after enough time.

00:08:59.416 --> 00:09:04.791
My idea was to return ascending
 or descending order using a subquery.

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:09.166
So I would check
 if the first character of the flag is A.

00:09:09.166 --> 00:09:12.708
If it was, my subquery
 would return ascending.

00:09:13.125 --> 00:09:15.458
If it wasn't, it would return descending.

00:09:16.041 --> 00:09:19.416
In most SQL databases, there are functions

00:09:19.416 --> 00:09:22.416
like if and substring
 that will make it possible.

00:09:23.625 --> 00:09:25.666
I could easily observe if

00:09:25.666 --> 00:09:29.083
it's ascending or descending
 by looking at the response.

00:09:29.583 --> 00:09:34.875
If it was descending, then I knew that
 the first character of my flag was not A.

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:37.375
So I tried to B, C and so on.

00:09:38.416 --> 00:09:41.250
After I finally saw ascending order,

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:45.958
I knew the first character of the flag
 so I could move on to the second one.

00:09:46.791 --> 00:09:49.833
I would repeat this
 until I got the whole flag.

00:09:50.958 --> 00:09:53.166
Of course, this process is not manual.

00:09:54.541 --> 00:09:58.583
The problem was I didn't find a way
 to return ascending

00:09:58.583 --> 00:10:02.416
or descending from a subquery
 as those are not strings.

00:10:03.666 --> 00:10:07.708
So then I started to think
 what can be after the order clause.

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:10.375
The answer was the limit clause 

00:10:10.375 --> 00:10:13.291
which I used at the beginning
 but then forgot about.

00:10:14.208 --> 00:10:18.166
It tells the database
 how many records we want to return.

00:10:19.708 --> 00:10:21.458
I easily modified 

00:10:21.458 --> 00:10:25.916
my subquery to return 0 or 1 inside
 the limit clause.

00:10:26.833 --> 00:10:28.875
It did work locally. 

00:10:28.875 --> 00:10:31.000
However, it's a primitive solution.

00:10:31.333 --> 00:10:33.375
It generates a lot of traffic.

00:10:34.708 --> 00:10:38.583
For each character
 of the encrypted and encoded flag,

00:10:38.625 --> 00:10:42.583
I needed to send 64 requests
 in the worst case.

00:10:43.666 --> 00:10:46.416
Considering the output had the IV,

00:10:46.416 --> 00:10:50.208
the ciphertext and the auth tag,
 it could take very long.

00:10:50.708 --> 00:10:54.333
I think the real flag
 had about 200 characters.

00:10:55.833 --> 00:10:58.125
If I were playing the CTF for points,

00:10:58.125 --> 00:11:01.916
I would likely just leave the script
 running for a few hours.

00:11:03.041 --> 00:11:07.541
But I didn't. Instead,
 I wanted to have a bit more fun.

00:11:08.666 --> 00:11:12.000
So I decided to create
 an efficient exploit,

00:11:12.166 --> 00:11:17.166
especially that I saw that the challenge
 was yet to be solved by anyone.

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:20.375
Usual tricks you can 

00:11:20.375 --> 00:11:25.333
use to speed up blind SQL injection
 exfiltration are binary search,

00:11:25.333 --> 00:11:28.541
which reduces it
 to 6 requests per character,

00:11:29.166 --> 00:11:32.875
using multiprocessing to brute force
 many characters at once

00:11:33.208 --> 00:11:36.500
or using regexes,
 combining those two techniques.

00:11:38.041 --> 00:11:40.833
But I knew I could do it
 even quicker than this.

00:11:42.333 --> 00:11:45.125
Considering that
 we are in the limit clause,

00:11:45.125 --> 00:11:48.458
we are not restricted to returning 0 or 1.

00:11:49.083 --> 00:11:51.833
It can be any number. 

00:11:51.833 --> 00:11:53.916
My idea was to add 

00:11:53.916 --> 00:11:58.416
128 notes and return the amount of notes

00:11:58.750 --> 00:12:02.750
equal to ASCII code
 of the next character of the flag.

00:12:03.916 --> 00:12:06.708
So when the first character was capital E

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:11.875
with ASCII code 69,
 then my query would return 69 notes

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:16.708
because my limit clause
 would be 69 as a result of this subquery.

00:12:18.166 --> 00:12:20.708
To see what was the
 value of the limit clause,

00:12:20.750 --> 00:12:24.250
I could just count the amount of notes
 in the response.

00:12:25.458 --> 00:12:28.125
I did overcomplicate this part a bit.

00:12:28.750 --> 00:12:31.750
Instead of counting notes,
 I named them LIMIT0

00:12:31.750 --> 00:12:36.416
to LIMIT127, which caused some boring

00:12:36.416 --> 00:12:39.625
and annoying problems,
 but I somehow dealt with them.

00:12:40.791 --> 00:12:45.458
Finally, I successfully tested my script
 on the local instance,

00:12:45.458 --> 00:12:48.583
and I was ready to smash
 the live environment.

00:12:49.333 --> 00:12:52.000
My approach meant that I only needed

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:56.083
128 requests at the beginning to add notes

00:12:56.291 --> 00:13:00.625
and then one request
 per each character of the encrypted flag.

00:13:02.208 --> 00:13:05.250
It meant that with 200 characters flag,

00:13:05.500 --> 00:13:09.000
I only needed 328 requests.

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:14.041
That's a lot less than 1200 requests
 of binary search

00:13:14.208 --> 00:13:17.500
and 12,800 of native solution.

00:13:18.666 --> 00:13:22.375
That's the worst case,
 but even if we take half of that number

00:13:22.375 --> 00:13:27.625
as an average complexity,
 my solution was still 95% faster.

00:13:28.583 --> 00:13:30.958
The server had some latency issues,

00:13:30.958 --> 00:13:34.916
as probably many players were brute
 forcing simultaneously.

00:13:35.208 --> 00:13:38.416
But after some time,
 I finally got the flag.

00:13:39.625 --> 00:13:43.458
Thanks to the fact that I invested
 a bit more time offline

00:13:43.583 --> 00:13:47.416
to prepare my exploit,
 I saved a lot of time online.

00:13:48.041 --> 00:13:52.250
That's probably the reason why I was
 the first one to solve the challenge.

